/-
# Kourovka Notebook 21, Problem 38

KOU-21.38: does there exist a group with spread exactly 1?

The spread of `G` is the greatest `k` such that any `k` nontrivial elements
simultaneously generate `G` with a suitable common partner `y`; spread
exactly 1 means this holds for single elements but fails for pairs (hence
for every `m > 1`, since a failing pair extends to an `m`-tuple).
-/
module

public import Mathlib.Algebra.Group.Subgroup.Basic
public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted

@[expose] public section

universe u

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.Kourovka21Problem38Wanted

/-! Source record `UM-00002547-Q001` (KOU-21.38). -/

/-- **Spread at least $k$.** A group $G$ has spread at least $k$, for a natural number $k$, when for
every family $x_1, \dots, x_k$ of nontrivial elements of $G$, not necessarily distinct, there is an
element $y \in G$ such that the subgroup $\langle x_i, y \rangle$ generated by $x_i$ and $y$ is
all of $G$ for every $i$. -/
def HasSpreadAtLeast (G : Type u) [Group G] (k : ℕ) : Prop :=
  ∀ x : Fin k → G, (∀ i, x i ≠ 1) →
    ∃ y : G, ∀ i, Subgroup.closure {x i, y} = ⊤

/-- **Spread exactly $k$.** A group $G$ has spread exactly $k$, for a natural number $k$, when $G$
has spread at least $k$ and, for every natural number $m > k$, $G$ does not have spread at
least $m$. -/
def HasSpreadExactly (G : Type u) [Group G] (k : ℕ) : Prop :=
  HasSpreadAtLeast G k ∧ ∀ m : ℕ, m > k → ¬ HasSpreadAtLeast G m

/-- **Existence of a group of spread exactly one (Kourovka 21.38).** The conjecture that there
exists a group of spread exactly $1$. -/
def conjecture : Prop :=
  ∃ (G : Type u) (_ : Group G), HasSpreadExactly G 1

-- Carried source audit `UM-00002547-Q001` (see the Moved/ record).

/--
**There is a group of spread exactly one.** There exists a group $G$ of spread exactly $1$:
for every nontrivial $x \in G$ there is $y \in G$ with $\langle x, y \rangle = G$, but for every
natural number $m > 1$ there are nontrivial elements $x_1, \dots, x_m \in G$ such that no single
$y \in G$ satisfies $\langle x_i, y \rangle = G$ for all $i$. This answers Kourovka Problem 21.38
affirmatively.

The Nilradical note A group of spread one (17 September 2026, Kourovka Notebook site), Theorem 1,
shows that the subgroup $\{f \in F : f'(0^+) = f'(1^-)\}$ of Thompson's group $F$ is infinite and
has spread exactly one. The note is not refereed; the Kourovka Notebook (arXiv:1401.0300v49) marks
Problem 21.38 solved (AI solution confirmed by S. Corson).

Source: Nilradical, A group of spread one (note of 17 September 2026, Kourovka Notebook
site),
https://kourovkanotebookorg.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/nilradical-kourovka-21-38.pdf;
E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (20 October 2026), Problem 21.38,
https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v49. Moved from
`OpenConjectures/GroupTheory/Kourovka21Problem38`.
-/
public theorem_wanted conjecture_holds : conjecture

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.Kourovka21Problem38Wanted
